 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Provides that the department of taxation and finance shall withdraw from county records a tax lien for which the tax debt has been paid in full and satisfied.",
 "title": "Provides that the department of taxation and finance shall withdraw from county records a tax lien for which the tax debt has been paid in full and satisfied",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A3965",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A3965-2021",
     "printNo": "A3965",
     "session": 2021,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 63\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 3965, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the tax law, in relation to the withdrawal of tax\n      liens upon payment of taxes due\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would require the Department of Taxation and  Finance  (DTF)\nto  withdraw warrants for the collection of income tax debts from county\nrecords when the underlying tax debts have been paid. DTF would also  be\nrequired  to  notify  tax  debtors  when warrants have been withdrawn so\nthose debtors can inform credit reporting bureaus of the debt  satisfac-\ntion  and ask that the debts be removed from their credit reports. Nota-\nbly, though, the underlying tax debts would still be public because  the\ndebt  satisfaction  documents  DTF  must  file when debts are paid would\nremain in county records.\n \n  According to its sponsors, this bill is necessary to ensure  that  tax\ndebtors  are not negatively impacted by having tax debts listed on their\ncredit reports after the debts have been paid. While that may have  been\nan  issue  when  this legislation was first introduced in 2012, New York\ntax warrants have not appeared on credit reports since  2017,  when  New\nYork's  Attorney  General  and  a  coalition  of state attorneys general\nreached a negotiated settlement with the three  major  credit  reporting\ncompanies  that  limits  the  public records they can collect and use in\ncredit reports.\n \n  Finally, in order to implement the requirements of  this  bill  if  it\nwere enacted, DTF would have 60 days to develop new automated systems to\nwithdraw  the  more  than  100,000 personal income tax warrants that are\nsatisfied each year and to generate the required  notices  to  taxpayers\nonce those warrants have been withdrawn. County clerks would be required\nto  do the actual work of withdrawing the warrants from their records, a\nprocess that is manual in most counties. The bill does not identify  any\nnew state or local funding streams to meet these mandates.\n \n  I  understand  the concern of preventing former tax debtors from being\nnegatively impacted on credit reports, but given the negotiated  settle-\nment  mitigating that concern and because the bill would impose substan-\ntial new unfunded burdens on DTF and on  local  county  clerks,  without\nproviding  sufficient  time  or the necessary resources to implement its\nmandates, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  This bill is disapproved.                      (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 63,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2021
   }
